WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.520
What do you think? Can we only learn through&nbsp;
direct experience, or also from studying others?

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.920
Psychologist Albert Bandura came&nbsp;
up with an interesting experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.920
to answer that exact question and form a theory.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.920
Bandura came up with the social&nbsp;
learning theory in the 1960s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.680
a time dominated by scholars who&nbsp;
didn’t believe Bandura's idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.960
and argued that learning was always the result&nbsp;
of classical and operational conditioning.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.840
To prove that children can&nbsp;
learn by mere observation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.560
Bandura came up with the Bobo Doll&nbsp;
Experiments - a set of controversial studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.720
which involved preschool children,&nbsp;
adult models and a stand-up punch doll.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.280
In the first set of experiments, the children were&nbsp;
seated alone in a corner of the room. From there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:57.840
they observed an adult actor aggressively kicking&nbsp;
and scolding the Bobo doll for about 10 minutes.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.040
Afterwards, the same child was put&nbsp;
into a new playroom where another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.360
adult deliberately frustrated the child by&nbsp;
taking away the toys the child played with.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.400
The frustrated child was then put back into the&nbsp;
room with the Bobo doll where Bandura and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.200
team made the following 3 observations:
1. Children who initially saw the adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.800
punching the doll, often copied the behavior.
2. Boys were three times more likely to do so.&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:30.240
3. Boys reproduced the behavior twice as often,&nbsp;
if they observed a man do it compared to boys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.720
who observed a female actor. Girls were&nbsp;
also more influenced by same-sex models.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.520
To find out if movies would have the same effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.680
Bandura let some children watch the&nbsp;
model live, and others watch it on video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.320
or as a cartoon animation. As all&nbsp;
three groups showed similar responses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.840
Bandura concluded that children imitate others&nbsp;
regardless of where they have seen the behavior.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.200
In the last variation of the experiments,&nbsp;
Bandura wanted to know if the children would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.400
act differently when the models were reinforced&nbsp;
or punished for their behavior towards the doll.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.600
Now, the children first&nbsp;
saw the actor hit the doll,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.040
and then observed another adult enter the room.&nbsp;
This second adult would then react to the actor.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.440
These last results showed that it did not make&nbsp;
much of a difference to the children whether the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.480
aggressor was praised or not. However, the kids&nbsp;
who saw that the model was punished, displayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.840
much less aggression later, an effect which&nbsp;
was especially true for girls. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.400
seeing others being rewarded does not&nbsp;
necessarily motivate us to copy their behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.280
Seeing others being punished, on the other hand,&nbsp;
can significantly diminish our will to copy it.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.000
The fact that Bandura was able to prove that&nbsp;
children also learn by observing others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.080
was a breakthrough in psychology.&nbsp;
As a consequence of his findings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.640
scholars and government officials argued&nbsp;
that we should ban violence in films and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.440
games. Others suggested that the Bobo Doll&nbsp;
studies are not studies of aggression,but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.640
rather show that the children are simply motivated&nbsp;
by the desire to please adults or act like them.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.480
Bandura and his co-author&nbsp;
Richard Walters later defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.520
five key tenets of the Social Learning Theory:

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.000
1. Learning is a cognitive process&nbsp;
that takes place in a social setting.&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.720
2. Learning can occur by observing a&nbsp;
behavior AND the consequences of such&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.280
3. Learning can happen without an observable&nbsp;
change in behavior, because we may learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.000
without displaying what we have learned.
4. Reinforcements play a role but are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.520
not entirely responsible for learning.
5. Cognition, behavior, and environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.920
all mutually influence each other — a&nbsp;
process called reciprocal determinisms.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.560
Bandura, who in college attended a&nbsp;
psychology course only to kill time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.600
soon became passionate about the topic and then&nbsp;
one of most cited psychologists in history.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.520
About reality, he said: "Most of the images&nbsp;
of reality on which we base our actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.560
are really based on vicarious experience."

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.240
What do you think? How much of your&nbsp;
learning comes from observing others?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.840
And if you learn socially, who&nbsp;
are your greatest teachers?

